1. DEFINITIONS

   "Permission": A unilateral written authorization granted by the Author, including but not limited to signed declarations, formal email communications, or other verifiable written methods, permitting specific use of the Software.
   "Commercial Authorization Agreement (CAA)": A bilateral, legally binding written contract, mutually accepted and executed by both the Author and the User, defining the specific terms, scope, conditions, limitations, and financial obligations relating strictly to the Commercial Use of the Software. A CAA cannot be established for Non-Commercial Use. 
   For a CAA to be valid, it must be recognizable and enforceable as a contract under the laws of the country in which the Author is legally domiciled. To the extent permitted by applicable law, contracts formed through electronic signatures, digital signatures, explicit acceptance by email, or other legally recognized digital agreement methods shall be considered valid CAAs.
   "Software": Any source code, binaries, documentation, or related materials distributed under this License.
   "Author": The individual or legally recognized entity holding the copyright and other intellectual property rights of the Software.
   "User": Any individual, legally recognized entity, or Informal Group that uses, modifies, distributes, or otherwise interacts with the Software.
   "Commercial Use": Any use, deployment, or integration of the Software in a product, environment, or service offered for compensation. This includes, but is not limited to, paid services, subscription models, licensing, resale, or revenue-generating activities. 
   - In case of ambiguity, the classification of use as Commercial or Non-Commercial shall be determined based on the primary purpose of the Software's deployment, not its incidental outcomes.
   - Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) or cloud deployments shall be considered Commercial Use ONLY IF the service itself is offered for a fee or generates direct revenue. Incidental advertisements placed strictly by third-party free hosting providers do not constitute Commercial Use.
   - Advertising revenue generated from platforms, websites, or services under the User's direct control constitutes direct revenue and therefore unequivocally qualifies as Commercial Use.
   - Sponsorship alone shall not constitute Commercial Use unless it is construed as a commercial transaction for services or products under applicable statutory law.
   "Adaptation and Modification (AM)": Any modification, adaptation, derivative work, or reorganization of the Software. This includes additions, removals, alterations, refactoring, optimization, bug fixes, patches, forks, integrations, packaging, porting, or any other technical modification.
   "Informal Group": An unincorporated entity, hackathon team, or collective without separate legal personality. Where a CAA is executed on behalf of an Informal Group, it must contain the signature of the Author and at least one explicitly named representative of the group. Any member of an Informal Group not explicitly identified within the CAA shall have no rights to engage in Commercial Use under this License.
   "Software Support": Any technical assistance, maintenance, updates, or bug fixes provided by the Author.

